WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:06.980
For well over a decade,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.520
the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.610
has brought the comic
world to the big screen

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.450
using state-of-the-art visual effects

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.290
and elaborate stunts.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.240
Making a Marvel movie is
physically demanding work

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.800
for the actors and the
people behind the cameras.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.010
To learn how these movies come to life,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.110
let's take a look at
what eight Marvel movies

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.803
looked like behind the scenes.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.740
The third "Captain
America" movie went big.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.360
It was the first film ever
to employ IMAX 2D cameras,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.550
which came in handy during

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.120
the now legendary airport battle.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.400
For one daring jump,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.410
a stunt performer attached to a rope

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.130
leaped right behind Bucky
to capture this shot.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.150
For another shot,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.530
they actually flipped a truck over.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.030
The sequence where Cap
faces off against Crossbones

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.510
and falls out of a window

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.470
and then onto an awning, a truck,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:57.930
and then finally the ground

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.460
took a lot of planning.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.370
Stunt coordinator Sam Hargrave,

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.320
who also doubled for Cap at times,

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.180
said pulling this off
involved constructing

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.230
an awning and shipping dock
for the existing building.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.430
The awning had to be built
at just the right angle,

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.763
and all the pads had to be obscured.

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:22.050
"Doctor Strange" took the
MCU visually to a new level.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.018
The multiverse involved intense CGI work.

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.630
Framestore alone worked on

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.960
365 visual effects shots for the film.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.710
So, naturally, there was a lot of

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.350
green screen and empty spaces on set,

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.690
like when Doctor Strange
walks through this portal

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.240
into another dimension.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.810
On set, he was really walking
through what looked like

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.325
a giant glowing Hula-Hoop.

00:01:42.325 --> 00:01:44.250
Meanwhile, elaborate wire work

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.663
was used to give the appearance

00:01:45.663 --> 00:01:48.350
the characters were
floating through the air.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.840
And when Strange takes on his astral form,

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.300
the visual effects team
backlit everything.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.930
This helped them more easily create

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.870
a transparent effect.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:59.830
And what about Strange's
fateful car crash?

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.890
According to director Scott Derrickson,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.910
they had to destroy about
nine real Lamborghinis

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.536
for this one scene.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.130
Unsurprisingly, outer space

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.160
in the sequel to "Guardians of the Galaxy"

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.530
looked a lot like a blue screen.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.390
One of the most subtle touches in the film

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.100
was the de-aging done to Kurt Russell

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.570
for the opening flashback.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.380
While they did do some makeup touch-ups

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.460
to make him look younger,

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.380
it was largely CGI.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.380
A young actor was used for
reference of 30-year-old skin

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.270
and stood in for Russell on set.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:33.970
Visual effects artists later grafted

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.080
some of the stand-in's
skin onto the older actor.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.580
And how do you act alongside

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.000
a CGI character like Baby Groot?

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.560
With this Baby Groot doll,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.098
which gave the cast a better idea

00:02:44.098 --> 00:02:45.510
of how they should make eye contact

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.970
with Baby Groot in a given scene.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.010
Actors Bradley Cooper and Vin Diesel

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.450
recorded the voices of
Rocket and Baby Groot

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:53.490
in a sound booth.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.730
Their facial expressions and gestures

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:58.220
were also recorded and used for reference

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.953
when animating the characters.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.772
In "Black Panther," the
cast's rigorous training

00:03:06.772 --> 00:03:09.847
in fighting styles ranging
from judo to jujitsu

00:03:09.847 --> 00:03:12.090
is on full display throughout the film.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.240
Actor Michael B. Jordan

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.320
was covered in about
3,000 prosthetic dots.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.030
Each one was made with
a glue-like material.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.340
The makeup team had to make sure

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.460
they wouldn't all come off

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.180
during the Warrior Falls sequence,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:24.900
given that it was a fight scene involving

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.245
a lot of body contact and splashing water.

00:03:28.245 --> 00:03:30.670
"Black Panther" was
shot all over the world.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.470
Many of the Wakanda scenes were filmed

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.260
on a soundstage in Atlanta,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.520
while the car chase through
the streets of Busan

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.700
was actually filmed in South Korea.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.200
According to cinematographer
Rachel Morrison,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.450
they wanted to distinguish
Wakanda visually

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.070
from the rest of the world.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:47.250
One way they did that: lighting.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.740
For the Wakanda scenes,

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.650
Morrison said they relied
on warm afternoon light

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.653
and used supplemental light
to get rid of any shadows.

00:03:58.877 --> 00:04:01.770
"Avengers: Infinity War"
contains approximately

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:05.740
2,900 visual effects shots.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.310
Thanos is a motion-capture marvel.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:11.160
Bringing a CGI character
with over 40 minutes

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.670
of screen time to life
was a big challenge.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.110
At times, actor Josh Brolin would wear

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.470
a Thanos head on top of his own head

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.090
so the actors could use
this eyeline as a guide.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.040
Brolin wore a motion-capture suit,

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.477
tracking marks on his face,

00:04:24.477 --> 00:04:26.530
and a helmet with cameras attached to it

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.943
to get every subtle mannerism.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:36.280
In this sequel, Scott
Lang fought alongside

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.760
Hope Van Dyne, aka the Wasp.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.590
She made a big impression early on

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.325
during this fight
sequence in a restaurant.

00:04:43.325 --> 00:04:45.360
The stunt team went
through four to five weeks

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.940
of intense training for it.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.650
Evangeline Lilly worked with them

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.050
to tweak the character's moves

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.405
to fit the actress' own movements.

00:04:52.405 --> 00:04:54.850
For example, Lilly thought to add a lot of

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.458
S curves into her fighting style.

00:04:57.458 --> 00:05:00.694
Giant-Man was, of
course, created with CGI.

00:05:00.694 --> 00:05:03.280
But for the part where he
collapses into the water,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.770
they had sprinklers on set
to actually splash onlookers.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.590
Then there is the stunning quantum realm.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.630
When building this microscopic world,

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.130
the visual effects team made sure

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.270
to give it a psychedelic feel.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.920
Everything in this environment
was constantly in motion.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.540
To make such a tiny world
seem so much bigger,

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.210
they relied heavily on macrophotography.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.801
Other photography devices
such as lens flares

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:28.993
added to the energetic visuals.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:36.140
Oscar winner Brie Larson
got in superhero shape

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.050
to portray the titular hero.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.550
Larson worked with celebrity
trainer Jason Walsh

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.560
for nine months before "Captain
Marvel" started shooting.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.130
By the end of their time together,

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.470
she was strong enough to
be able to push a Jeep,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.330
so she was more than
ready for shots like this.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.470
And how do you make Captain Marvel fly?

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.880
Sometimes they used wires.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.460
Other times they used a tuning-fork rig,

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.870
which was operated by
crew members in blue suits

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:01.657
who would be edited out later.

00:06:01.657 --> 00:06:04.620
"Captain Marvel" also
features some of the MCU's

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.130
most convincing de-aging work to date.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.680
The visual effects team was tasked

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.050
with making Samuel L. Jackson

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.270
look like his 1990s self.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.770
Nick Fury has a lot of screen time.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.960
There are at least 800
de-aging shots in the film,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.140
and the artists had to take

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.610
nearly three decades off of him.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.990
While filming, Jackson donned a wig

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.920
and tracking dots on his face.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.370
In postproduction,

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:31.007
they got to work reshaping and
resculpting the actor's face

00:06:31.007 --> 00:06:33.280
while altering the texture of his skin.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.800
Luckily, they had a lot
of reference material

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.853
of 1990s-era Jackson to work off of.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.010
To play the updated version of Thor,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.650
chiseled star Chris
Hemsworth wore a bodysuit,

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.550
which he estimated
weighed 60 to 70 pounds,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.360
as well as a mouthpiece
that puffed out his cheeks.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.990
While the visual effects
team did some touch-ups,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.370
Marvel Entertainment visual
effects producer Jen Underdahl

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.560
said that actually having the suit on set

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.520
helped Hemsworth's performance.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.960
And for this one shot,

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.940
you will notice the
actors weren't actually

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.160
wearing their nanotech suits on set.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.900
That is because it made more
sense to create these suits

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.840
largely using visual effects.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.050
Since they constantly expand and shrink,

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.380
it would be hard to do that practically.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.920
Nothing compared to the
film's final battle.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.390
Bringing this many characters
together in one place

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.440
was a logistical challenge.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.330
Not every actor could
actually be there on set

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:29.670
at the same time.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.340
Take this shot, where the MCU's

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:33.030
female heroes stood together.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.530
Tom Holland couldn't be there on that day.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:37.900
Because Peter Parker hands Captain Marvel

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.380
the Infinity Gauntlet,

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:40.780
they needed to have somebody there

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.570
to interact with Brie Larson.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:44.560
So another actor stood in for him,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.333
and Holland was later
digitally added into the scene.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:51.907
Thanks for watching.

00:07:51.907 --> 00:07:52.905
If you liked this video,

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:54.840
subscribe for more just like it.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.560
Let us know what other Marvel movies

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.193
you wanna know more about in the comments.

